---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro'
import Capture from '../../components/Capture.astro'
import { doc, shot } from '../../lib/content'
const sections: [string, string][] = [
  ['prerequisites', 'Before you begin'],
  ['install', '1. Install the provider'],
  ['sign-in', '2. Add an account'],
  ['verify', '3. Test and select'],
  ['multiple-accounts', '4. Add more accounts'],
  ['routing', '5. Choose routing'],
  ['troubleshooting', 'Troubleshooting'],
]
---

<Guide
  title="Claude Code and multiple accounts"
  description="Use your Claude subscription through pi-claude-cli, verify the provider, and choose which account new sessions use."
  sections={sections}
>
  <aside class="notice">
    <strong>About these screenshots</strong><p>
      These are captures of Phosphor’s actual settings UI in the isolated demo harness. Account
      names, masked addresses, versions, and usage are example data. No personal accounts or
      credentials were loaded, including in the full-size images.
    </p>
  </aside>
  <section id="prerequisites">
    <h2>Before you begin</h2>
    <p>
      Complete <a href="/guides/#install">Phosphor and pi installation</a>. You also need the
      official Claude Code CLI and a Claude plan that permits the usage you intend. This guide
      describes the Pro/Max route; provider terms, limits, and any extra-usage charges still apply.
    </p>
    <p>
      Install Claude Code using its <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/setup"
        >official setup instructions for your platform</a
      >. Confirm <code>claude --version</code> works. Phosphor’s current integration requires <code
        >pi-claude-cli ≥ 0.7.1</code
      > and Claude Code <code>2.1.263+</code>; use a current compatible package. Provider 0.8.0+
      also supplies richer tool-result details.
    </p>
    <p>
      The provider drives the official CLI. Do not extract a Claude subscription token or paste one
      into pi’s API-key settings.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="install">
    <h2>1. Install the pi provider</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open Settings with Cmd+, on macOS or Ctrl+, on Windows/Linux.</li><li>
        Select <strong>Extensions</strong>. Under Recommended, find <strong
          >Claude Code provider</strong
        > and choose <strong>Add</strong>. If the card says it needs the Claude CLI on your PATH,
        install the CLI and make it visible to Phosphor before testing the provider.
      </li><li>
        Wait for the installation job to succeed. The nested <strong>Claude Code</strong> settings tab
        appears when the package is present.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>The terminal equivalent is:</p><pre
      tabindex="0"
      aria-label="Install the Claude Code provider"><code>pi install npm:@saccolabs/pi-claude-cli</code></pre>
    <Capture
      image={shot('guide-extensions')}
      alt="Extensions settings showing the recommended package catalog in a demo configuration."
      caption="Settings → Extensions: find the Claude Code provider"
      provenance="Settings demo · example data"
    />
    <p>
      Use a global install for a first setup. A project-scoped installation belongs to that
      workspace. Avoid conflicting global and project provider versions. Finish existing work and
      start a new session after installing or updating a package.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="sign-in">
    <h2>2. Add your first Claude account</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open <strong>Settings → Extensions → Claude Code</strong>.</li><li>
        Check the Health rows: the extension must be installed and the <code>claude</code> binary found.
      </li><li>
        If your existing Claude CLI login is listed, check that it is the intended account.
        Otherwise choose <strong>Add account</strong>.
      </li><li>
        Complete Claude’s sign-in in your browser. If Phosphor asks for a code, paste the code shown
        by Claude into <strong>Paste code</strong> and select Continue. Never share this code.
      </li><li>
        Confirm the account row appears. Use <strong>Sign in again</strong> to renew an expired login.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <Capture
      image={shot('guide-claude')}
      alt="Claude Code settings showing Health and two fictional accounts labeled Account A and Account B, with addresses masked."
      caption="Health and Accounts live in the Claude Code tab"
      provenance="Settings demo · masked examples"
    />
    <p>
      <strong>Two different account surfaces:</strong> the top-level Accounts tab manages pi’s provider
      sign-ins. The nested Claude Code tab manages the CLI-backed Claude logins used by this package.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="verify">
    <h2>3. Test the provider, then select it</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Scroll to <strong>Prove it end to end</strong> and choose <strong>Test provider</strong>.
      </li><li>
        Inspect the job output. A successful round trip shows <code>phosphor-provider-ok</code> and a
        confirmation message. This sends a small real prompt and uses plan quota.
      </li><li>
        Start a new session. In the composer’s model menu, choose a Claude model under <code
          >pi-claude-cli</code
        >.
      </li><li>
        Send a small prompt. Open the context meter to check which Claude account the session is
        using.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Leave the context-window setting at its default initially. Increasing it allows longer
      histories before compaction, but can increase the amount of context re-read per request. It
      does not increase your plan’s allowance.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="multiple-accounts">
    <h2>4. Add another authorized account</h2>
    <p>
      In the same Claude Code tab, choose <strong>Add account</strong> again. Each login is kept separately,
      rather than overwriting the previous one.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the browser immediately signs you into the first account, sign out at claude.ai before
      starting the second login. Signing in with an address already listed renews that row; it does
      not create another account.
    </p>
    <p>
      Only add accounts you are authorized to use. Routing organizes legitimate usage across them;
      it does not remove provider limits or change account terms. Choose <strong
        >Refresh usage</strong
      > to update the figures used for the next routing decision.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="routing">
    <h2>5. Choose how new sessions are routed</h2>
    <Capture
      image={shot('guide-routing')}
      alt="The Route new sessions controls with One account, In order top to bottom, and Round robin options; all accounts are fictional."
      caption="Routing applies to new sessions, not an account swap mid-turn"
      provenance="Settings demo · masked examples"
    />
    <ul>
      <li><strong>One account:</strong> choose the account that should bill new sessions.</li><li>
        <strong>In order, top to bottom:</strong> choose the first eligible account in the list. Use the
        arrows to change priority. Accounts reported unavailable, exhausted, or spending overage credits
        can be held back.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Round robin:</strong> rotate new sessions across eligible accounts rather than always
        choosing the first.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Quota readings may be cached. Missing or stale telemetry is not proof that an account has
      capacity. Check its usage and provider billing settings when the numbers matter.
    </p>
    <aside class="notice">
      <strong>No silent mid-session failover</strong><p>
        A running session keeps the account assigned at spawn. Open an account row to inspect its
        usage and live sessions. Moving a lane to another account respawns it from saved history;
        the next turn re-reads that history and can incur additional context cost. Let the current
        turn finish before moving it.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>
  <section id="troubleshooting">
    <h2>Troubleshooting and updates</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Claude Code tab missing:</strong> confirm the provider is installed in the applicable
        scope, then reopen settings.
      </li><li>
        <strong>CLI not found:</strong> use Health to check the path. A desktop launch may not inherit
        a terminal-only Node or shell setup.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Package or CLI is old:</strong> use the separate Update controls. The terminal equivalents
        are <code>pi update npm:@saccolabs/pi-claude-cli</code> and <code>claude update</code>.
        Updating one does not update the other.
      </li><li>
        <strong>A new model is missing:</strong> the provider’s model catalog comes from pi, not the CLI.
        Update pi as well; a Claude CLI update alone is not enough.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Old session reports a context-policy change:</strong> use the offered <strong
          >Rebuild the Claude session</strong
        > action. The next turn imports saved history and incurs a full-context cache write.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Claude cannot see your existing MCP servers:</strong> Phosphor deliberately uses pi’s
        MCP adapter, not Claude’s independent MCP configuration. <a href="/guides/connectors/"
          >Configure those services in MCP Connectors</a
        >.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <a href={doc('cli-providers.md')}>Provider architecture and version requirements</a> · <a
        href={doc('settings.md#accounts')}>Account reference</a
      > · <a href="https://github.com/agustinsacco/pi-claude-cli">Provider source and releases</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
